WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.816
Three server primitives, three different deciders.

2
00:00:03.096 --> 00:00:05.421
Servers offer three kinds of capability.

3
00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:12.101
The MCP docs organize them by who controls them, and that is the most useful way to remember them.

4
00:00:12.381 --> 00:00:14.045
Tools are model-controlled.

5
00:00:14.325 --> 00:00:23.051
They are functions with a name, a description, and a JSON Schema for inputs, discovered with tools/list and invoked with tools/call.

6
00:00:23.331 --> 00:00:26.936
The model decides when to call one, based on the conversation.

7
00:00:27.216 --> 00:00:32.016
Tools can read or write: search flights, send a message, create a calendar event.

8
00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:45.992
Because a model can fire a tool without the user asking, the tools page says there should always be a human able to deny tool invocations, with clear indicators when tools run and confirmation prompts for operations.

9
00:00:46.272 --> 00:00:48.427
Resources are application-driven.

10
00:00:48.707 --> 00:00:54.744
They are data identified by URIs, such as file:///reports/q3.

11
00:00:55.024 --> 00:01:02.043
md or tracker://summary, and a server can publish URI templates for families of resources.

12
00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:08.083
The host decides how to use them: a picker the user browses, search, or automatic inclusion.

13
00:01:08.363 --> 00:01:10.709
Resources are context, not actions.

14
00:01:10.989 --> 00:01:12.504
Prompts are user-controlled.

15
00:01:12.784 --> 00:01:18.864
They are reusable templates with arguments that the user picks explicitly, typically as slash commands.

16
00:01:19.144 --> 00:01:22.707
The server writes the template; the user decides when to use it.

17
00:01:22.987 --> 00:01:29.685
A good prompt packages a workflow (“triage this issue using these tools”) so users don’t have to rediscover it.

18
00:01:29.965 --> 00:01:31.843
Two details matter in practice.

19
00:01:32.123 --> 00:01:41.573
First, many hosts support tools far better than resources or prompts, so check what your target host actually surfaces before designing around a primitive.

20
00:01:41.853 --> 00:01:44.669
Second, the control model tells you where risk lives.

21
00:01:44.949 --> 00:01:47.915
A resource the user attaches is the user’s choice.

22
00:01:48.195 --> 00:01:53.805
A tool call is the model’s choice, so tools are where your validation and approval effort goes.
